A||||8||Earne||T-Tops, worth it?||||5||
Z||000000||Earne||01-14-2000||05:40 AM||||I'd like to know what everyone thinks of T-tops.  I know, they are scarce, really great, car is worth more, etc.  That's not what I'm looking for.<P>I have a chance to pick up an in-the-box, brand spankin' new set including bags, locks, keys and the whole shootin' match.  The price is $2,000 and if I want them put in by a pro (professional that has installed dozens of sets both new and used), that will cost an extra $1,000.  Before you tell me how much money that is, I have an 86 1/2 silver GT that is near showroom.  I have made and installed the GM prototype side scoops (copied from pics) and chromed out the entire top end of the engine (incl. throttle body and coolant lines).  Lots of other tweeks incl. 17" 12 spoke wheels with low pro Pirelli's.  The car took second place in the Michigan Club's all Pontiac show last year.  It's on the bottom of pg 19 of the latest FOCOA mag.  I am going over the edge on this one?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||01-14-2000||09:09 AM||||It all depends on what you are after. If you want a bit of "wow" factor, and like the fresh air... Yeah. Go for it. Sounds like you have already created quite a show-piece. If I had it to do over again, I probably wouldn't have bought a T-top car. Just for the fact that replacement parts are so scarce. Since you have a chance to buy "brand new", this isn't going to be an issue for a while. They do seem to take away just a bit from the structural rigidity of the car. Will probably squeak a little. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||V6pack||01-14-2000||09:24 AM||||Earne, I can second what Raydar said about squeaks, rattles, etc..<P>On the other hand, I have two now, about to be three.  You can't beat them for a spring/summer/early fall car. I left my tops off all summer.<P>Sounds like you enjoy unique, so if your show car is going to be in the stable for a while and you don't want another Fiero... Go for it.<P>If there is room for another car. Pick up a T-top. I found 3 in six months. You can still find "average" ones for just a K or two more than your kit/install total.  I have an '87 GT-T I'll part with for 4.5K.<BR>My $0.02...<BR>V6pack||||reg||6
Z||000003||Tom88gt||01-14-2000||04:16 PM||||Search the junkyards.  I got the whole t-top roof section minus the headliner and dome light for $200.  It's not new but only a 1/10 the cost.  I bought it for spare parts but maybe in a couple of years, I may install it on a hard top.  $3000 for a complete, new package installed may or may not be reasonable (your money, your call).  But you can still find bargains if used is ok for you.<P>Personaly, you can probably find an original T-top for a lot less than $3000 extra.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Earne||01-14-2000||05:29 PM||||Guys, thanks much for the advice.  "Wow" is a factor, but I don't think much of squeeks and rattles.  I wonder if the rattles are the result of 11 plus years of use.  After all, the kit was riveted in aftermarket.  I'm proud of my car, took a lot of work.  I plan on buying other Fieros but I haven't found one with tops that I like or would pay the price for.  I am negotiating for an '84 Indy with 6000 miles.  Absolutely showroom.  Is $8,900 too much?<P>Thought I's share a pic of mine, hope this works<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=103583&a=1520894&p=15208075&Sequence=1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Earne (edited 01-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-14-2000||06:50 PM||||Unless your kit also includes the frame stiffening parts, which are Fiero Specific, then it's not complete and you will add even more to the 3K$ you are already spending.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||01-14-2000||07:46 PM||||Hey Earne, just pulled out my focoa mag and seeing your Fiero I wouldn't do it if it was mine. It's perfect now. I'd maybe have them installed in the Indy if you get it. Notice you have a trailer in the back for your show trips! Smart!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||01-15-2000||02:10 AM||||There are no "frame stiffening" parts on the T-Top Fieros.  The new roof is it.....nothing on the frame (other than the roof) is modified at all.  I've owned 4 T-Top Fieros.....believe me, there is no bracing on the frame (I've also had a few brand new kits, none had any either).<P>As far as the $3000 price goes.....that's really not bad for a new kit, but I too would recommend buying a Fiero with them already installed.  I paid $3500 for my Formula with factory T-Tops and I've seen them go for far less.  There is one on eBay (not sure if the auction is over or not) for less than $2000....it's an '88 Formula.<P>I have an '85 SE 4-cyl. with T-Tops (currently not running).....any offers? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-15-2000||08:32 AM||||I think T-tops are pretty cool, but I don't know if I'd bugger up an already nice car with them.  You can get too much "stuff" on a car.  Then one thing starts to detract from the whole package.  Just my opinion.||||reg||1
